WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.833
Industrial threat hunting is a proactive, defensive search for suspicious or unexplained activity.

00:00:06.833 --> 00:00:14.609
It differs from alert response by starting with a hypothesis rather than a generated signal.

00:00:14.609 --> 00:00:20.499
It remains grounded in safety, reliability, process integrity, and operational authority.

00:00:20.499 --> 00:00:32.987
Effective hunts depend on asset inventory, real architecture, expected communication, process modes, and telemetry from networks, protocols, identities, remote access, endpoints, historians, and controller changes.

00:00:32.987 --> 00:00:38.878
Every source has blind spots, so confidence must match actual coverage.

00:00:38.878 --> 00:00:42.412
Useful hypotheses are focused and testable.

00:00:42.412 --> 00:00:49.245
Hunts may examine unauthorized access, abnormal industrial communication, or engineering and configuration activity.

00:00:49.245 --> 00:00:57.963
Observed anomalies are leads rather than proof; maintenance, faults, failover, and legitimate work can produce similar evidence.

00:00:57.963 --> 00:01:03.854
Documentation preserves the plan, evidence, findings, confidence, operational explanation, and follow-up.

00:01:03.854 --> 00:01:14.457
Repeatable findings can become detections, while inventory corrections and monitoring gaps improve the environment even when a hypothesis is not supported.

00:01:14.457 --> 00:01:20.347
The goal is not to disrupt production or perform offensive experimentation.

00:01:20.347 --> 00:01:25.296
It is better visibility, earlier detection, and safer operations.

00:01:25.296 --> 00:01:33.071
Security hunters provide investigative discipline; operators and engineers provide process meaning and safe response guidance.

00:01:33.071 --> 00:01:37.548
Their collaboration turns uncertain observations into defensible action.
